WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.000
(upbeat rock music)

00:00:14.820 --> 00:00:18.270
- The mission of Improbable
AI Lab is to build robots

00:00:18.270 --> 00:00:20.460
which can perform the same physical tasks

00:00:20.460 --> 00:00:22.080
that humans perform today.

00:00:22.080 --> 00:00:24.960
The things that we want robots to be doing

00:00:24.960 --> 00:00:28.200
so humans can focus on
what they are good at,

00:00:28.200 --> 00:00:31.650
and what they want to do to
realize their full potential.

00:00:31.650 --> 00:00:35.430
In simulation, our robots are
learning by trial and error.

00:00:35.430 --> 00:00:36.960
They're essentially trying to figure out

00:00:36.960 --> 00:00:40.320
what sequence of commands
should they give to their legs.

00:00:40.320 --> 00:00:43.890
One fun task is for these
robots to play soccer,

00:00:43.890 --> 00:00:46.230
and definitely there
has been a lot of work

00:00:46.230 --> 00:00:48.180
in robots playing soccer,

00:00:48.180 --> 00:00:50.880
but a lot of this work
has been with robots

00:00:50.880 --> 00:00:53.040
which are not as agile or dynamic.

00:00:53.040 --> 00:00:55.740
- Legs robots are really
interesting to work with

00:00:55.740 --> 00:00:58.290
because they're a bit
challenging to control.

00:00:58.290 --> 00:00:59.760
To dribble a soccer ball,

00:00:59.760 --> 00:01:01.650
it's not sufficient for the robot to rely

00:01:01.650 --> 00:01:03.990
on what it feels through its motors.

00:01:03.990 --> 00:01:06.360
It also needs to know
where the soccer ball is,

00:01:06.360 --> 00:01:07.885
and there's not enough
information about that

00:01:07.885 --> 00:01:09.630
in just what the robot's feeling.

00:01:09.630 --> 00:01:10.650
It needs vision.

00:01:10.650 --> 00:01:12.870
It needs to be able to perceive
the ball from a distance

00:01:12.870 --> 00:01:15.720
and run to it and also kick it

00:01:15.720 --> 00:01:17.670
when it's sort of underneath the robot,

00:01:17.670 --> 00:01:20.400
even if it's moving in a
way that's not expected.

00:01:20.400 --> 00:01:23.298
For this, we actually
do leverage the cameras

00:01:23.298 --> 00:01:27.840
in the robot's head and body
to detect and perceive the ball

00:01:27.840 --> 00:01:30.030
and make decisions based
on that information.

00:01:30.030 --> 00:01:31.140
Our robot's having a problem,

00:01:31.140 --> 00:01:34.380
which is when it kicks the ball on grass,

00:01:34.380 --> 00:01:36.840
the ball slows down and
it recaptures the ball

00:01:36.840 --> 00:01:37.950
and it keeps dribbling.

00:01:37.950 --> 00:01:40.800
But when it kicks the ball on pavement,

00:01:40.800 --> 00:01:42.780
the ball just keeps you rolling forever,

00:01:42.780 --> 00:01:44.130
because it doesn't slow down.

00:01:44.130 --> 00:01:46.680
So we implemented a drag model

00:01:46.680 --> 00:01:49.590
to model how the robot
slows down on grass,

00:01:49.590 --> 00:01:52.860
but we need to modify it so
that the robot is capable

00:01:52.860 --> 00:01:55.890
of catching up when the ball's
rolling fast on pavement.

00:01:55.890 --> 00:01:58.710
I think it's fun to watch a
human interact with the robot,

00:01:58.710 --> 00:02:01.620
and the soccer ball is a
really good medium for that.

00:02:01.620 --> 00:02:03.870
- So what you're going to
see in many of these videos

00:02:03.870 --> 00:02:07.200
is robots dancing, going under things,

00:02:07.200 --> 00:02:11.850
going over obstacles, running
on ice, many different things.

00:02:11.850 --> 00:02:13.983
We don't have to go to each task

00:02:13.983 --> 00:02:18.150
and have a human do our design strategies

00:02:18.150 --> 00:02:20.100
which are specific to that task.

00:02:20.100 --> 00:02:23.151
All of these strategies are a result

00:02:23.151 --> 00:02:26.520
of the same way of training the robot.

00:02:26.520 --> 00:02:29.790
We train in simulation by trial and error,

00:02:29.790 --> 00:02:31.950
and then deploy them in the real world.

00:02:31.950 --> 00:02:33.300
- We're excited to continue our research

00:02:33.300 --> 00:02:35.311
on legged robotics to
unlock their potential

00:02:35.311 --> 00:02:38.190
to accomplish dynamic
tasks in their environment

00:02:38.190 --> 00:02:39.483
and assist humans.

